00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.440
and all future episodes, with the&nbsp;
CuriosityStream and Nebula bundle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.360
which is just $12/year right now&nbsp;
in the U.S. for Memorial Day.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.280
Just 100 miles, or 160 kilometers,&nbsp;
east across the narrow Taiwan Strait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.560
you could be forgiven for thinking China could one&nbsp;
day just swallow up its little disconnected tail.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:31.680
Russia has three times the population of&nbsp;
Ukraine. China, 60 times more than Taiwan.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:38.720
The Russian economy is — or, more accurately,&nbsp;
was — 9 and a half times larger than Ukraine’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.640
China’s is 22 times the size of Taiwan’s.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.040
The Russian landmass is 28&nbsp;
times larger than Ukraine’s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.520
And China’s, 265 times larger than Taiwan’s.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.040
In 2020, China spent $244 billion on defense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.560
Taiwan, just 11, about as much&nbsp;
as the Netherlands or Colombia.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.200
Of course, as the American Navy demonstrates, not&nbsp;
every dollar spent on defense is created equally.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.400
Still, the disparities are quite dramatic. China&nbsp;
has upwards of 2 million active duty troops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:24.000
thirty-three hundred fighters, and 59&nbsp;
subs to Taiwan’s 160,000, 560, and four.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:30.480
In other words: China is probably every bit&nbsp;
as big, strong, and powerful as it looks.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.320
And yet, ask just about anyone in the&nbsp;
defense community and they’ll tell you:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.320
a Chinese invasion of Taiwan&nbsp;
would be just downright hard.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.280
Now, there are many different ways China could go&nbsp;
about this — by launching a Cuba-style blockade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.720
by first seizing smaller islands on its&nbsp;
periphery, or even infiltrating from within.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.800
But the most likely sequence&nbsp;
of events would be as follows…

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.120
First, China would start bombing critical&nbsp;
Taiwanese infrastructure — ports, airports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.240
and bases — in hopes of causing chaos, confusion,&nbsp;
and a breakdown in the chain of command.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.520
Second, China would attempt to establish air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.400
superiority — control of the skies and&nbsp;
therefore cover for what comes next.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.800
As we’ve seen in Ukraine, this&nbsp;
is much harder than it sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.000
even for an ostensibly larger&nbsp;
and more powerful attacker.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.480
Still, up to this point,&nbsp;
there’s no doubt China could,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.240
at the very least, quickly inflict&nbsp;
serious and irreparable harm.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.600
This next phase is when the People’s&nbsp;
Liberation Army likely runs into trouble.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.920
As anyone who's seen “Saving&nbsp;
Private Ryan” can attest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.160
amphibious assaults are no walk in&nbsp;
the park, even on the best of days.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.360
Generally speaking, to be successful, they&nbsp;
require three conditions: air superiority,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:59.120
overwhelming fire and manpower or surprise,&nbsp;
and the ability to quickly reinforce.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.600
Every battle is different, of&nbsp;
course, but historical successes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.040
have, with few exceptions,&nbsp;
possessed all three criteria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.560
In the vast majority of cases, the absence&nbsp;
of even one of these has led to failure.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:19.360
If everything goes as planned for China, it&nbsp;
will have accomplished the first. But that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.560
still leaves two very difficult hurdles,&nbsp;
which brings us to the real challenge…

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:32.480
Russia’s struggles so far in Ukraine make it&nbsp;
easy to lose sight of the basics. This is not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:38.640
with one major exception, a geographically&nbsp;
challenging environment. When you come across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:45.040
pictures of the war, make a note of what you&nbsp;
see: roads — and what you don’t: mountains.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.880
We’re used to seeing Russia’s invasion on a map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.280
But this is what it looks like on the&nbsp;
ground — what geographers would call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:06:00.400
flat, Eurasian Steppe. The biggest natural&nbsp;
impediments are trees, grass, and… tractors.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.920
The coast of Taiwan, on the other hand, is much&nbsp;
trickier. Sure, there are flat mud plains and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.600
coastal rice paddies, but any invader would&nbsp;
soon be met with 2 and 3-thousand foot peaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:20.160
within artillery distance of the shoreline.&nbsp;
In between are fairly populous, urban areas.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:27.200
Take the usual defender’s advantage — 2&nbsp;
or 3 attackers per defending troop and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:34.000
thanks to Taiwan’s tall and irregular&nbsp;
coastline, multiply it several times over.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.320
If that sounds hard, well, the&nbsp;
world’s most powerful military agreed.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.880
The United States considered&nbsp;
attacking then-Japanese-occupied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:49.120
Taiwan in 1944 — going so far as to&nbsp;
draft plans for “Operation Causeway”.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.600
Overtaking a patchwork of 30,000 starving&nbsp;
Japanese defenders, it estimated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:03.120
would require an invasion force over twice the&nbsp;
size of D-Day — 400,000 marines and 4,000 ships.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.680
Despite commanding this&nbsp;
incredible concentration of force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.360
Pacific War planners considered the idea too&nbsp;
risky and opted for the Philippines instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:16.720
referring to Taiwan as an&nbsp;
“unsinkable aircraft carrier”.
 &nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Suffice to say, China would need a figurative&nbsp;
boatload of troops. Somewhere between 1 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.840
2 million, to be precise — making it the&nbsp;
largest amphibious operation in history.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.920
If war is primarily an exercise in logistics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:40.160
this would be the ultimate challenge. Moving&nbsp;
that many bodies simultaneously is no easy feat.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:47.680
Take a simple, back-of-the-envelope calculation,&nbsp;
for instance. Assuming China was using that same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:53.040
2020 equipment from earlier, this is all it&nbsp;
could transport across the Strait at a time.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:00.400
Even if all it transported were troops — it&nbsp;
could only move about 30,000 — or 3% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.760
total number of active PLA Ground&nbsp;
forces — at a time. And remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.440
that’s if they just carried&nbsp;
people — with no tanks or armor.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:15.920
For obvious reasons, this hypothetical operation&nbsp;
has been nicknamed the “million-man swim”.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.120
Oh! And by the way, nothing&nbsp;
about this would be a secret.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.880
Experts estimate the world&nbsp;
will have no fewer than 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:31.760
and up to 60 days of warning — giving Taiwan a&nbsp;
full 1-2 months to make itself as impenetrable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.880
as possible — laying mines, staging weapons,&nbsp;
and repositioning critical infrastructure.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:43.120
Not only that but we also already know during&nbsp;
which parts of the year China could attack.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.720
Waves are too strong in the Winter&nbsp;
and typhoons too unpredictable in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:53.840
Summer — leaving just two good&nbsp;
candidates — April or October.

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.120
So too do we know where it would happen.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:01.600
There are only about 13&nbsp;
beaches across all of Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.200
suitable for landing — and&nbsp;
“suitable” is being generous.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:12.160
Take one of these — Linkou beach, for instance.&nbsp;
Although invaders would be welcomed by about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:17.040
a mile of flatland, they would soon be&nbsp;
confronted by two huge mountain ranges.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.800
This would also be these PLA soldiers’&nbsp;
and generals’ first real experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.240
with combat. China hasn’t fought a&nbsp;
single major war for over 40 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.600
when it invaded Vietnam and&nbsp;
was quickly forced to withdraw.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.520
Now, even if it was ‘successful’,&nbsp;
China would now face a new challenge:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:43.360
occupying an island of 23 million people&nbsp;
— most of whom hate it — with much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.120
what it considers ‘valuable’&nbsp;
about Taiwan utterly destroyed.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.800
Military doctrine suggests a 1:10&nbsp;
ratio of soldiers to population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.520
for successful counterinsurgency operations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.760
which, on paper, would require the entire&nbsp;
PLA — leaving no one for anything else.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.240
As we see in Ukraine, local resistance can&nbsp;
be incredibly powerful, especially given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:13.360
that Taiwan has been preparing for its entire&nbsp;
modern existence for precisely this scenario.

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:20.720
In one 2020 poll, 77% of the Taiwanese public said&nbsp;
they were “willing to fight” for their country.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.880
Still, it should be noted that,&nbsp;
at least today, conscription&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:31.840
only lasts 4 months in Taiwan. How much&nbsp;
can one really do or learn in 120 days?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.960
And, in general, conscription armies&nbsp;
are less competent and more apathetic.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:46.080
But don’t get too lost in the weeds asking&nbsp;
whether China can attack before asking if it will.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.840
Balance of strength is important,&nbsp;
but it’s far from the whole picture.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.000
Any theory about what China wants or how it thinks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:57.520
has to contend with two&nbsp;
seemingly contradictory facts…

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.880
On one hand, China has been beating the drum of&nbsp;
war for seven decades, making countless explicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:11.520
threats and seemingly preparing its population&nbsp;
for conflict. Like North Korea and missiles, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:17.520
issue seems to percolate to the surface several&nbsp;
times a year. Each time feels like a crisis.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:25.920
Yet, on the other hand, China has…been beating the&nbsp;
drum of war for seven decades and has never once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.320
actually followed through. No&nbsp;
matter your political affiliations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.280
you can’t say China hasn’t had the full range&nbsp;
of American administrations to choose from.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.720
The funny thing is that, look at a graph&nbsp;
of Google searches for headlines related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:48.000
to war between Taiwan and China, and a clear&nbsp;
pattern emerges: Panic, at least by this metric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.600
is a relatively recent, and uniquely&nbsp;
English phenomenon. Searches in traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:59.120
Chinese — used in Taiwan — have been pretty&nbsp;
much steady for as long as the graph goes back.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:06.240
Likewise, when Chinese jets make mock attack&nbsp;
runs toward Taiwan, it’s very often the American,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:13.520
not Taiwanese public who panics. So, what do&nbsp;
calmer minds know that the rest of us don’t?

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:22.000
In democracies like the U.S., it’s often said that&nbsp;
each party needs the other. That, if one party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:28.080
were ever given too much power for too long, it&nbsp;
would be forced to deliver on its promises — many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:34.560
of which it never intended to. It would have no&nbsp;
excuse, no scapegoat to blame when it failed.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:42.080
A similar dynamic plays out in autocracies&nbsp;
like China. The enemy, in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:47.520
is not the opposing party — there are none —&nbsp;
but some manufactured national humiliation.

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.360
Remember! Taiwan, the island, was&nbsp;
never ruled by the Communist Party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.200
The framing of Taiwan as a&nbsp;
source of shame for China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:02.640
that it can only ever be “complete” after&nbsp;
quote “reunification” — is entirely artificial.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:07.600
China, in other words, wilfully&nbsp;
chose this version of history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:14.880
And its predictable effects give us a hint as to&nbsp;
why. Chinese citizens are regularly told their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:20.880
country could easily “take Taiwan in a day”,&nbsp;
constantly reminded that they, personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:27.040
are being transgressed upon when American carrier&nbsp;
groups sail through the Taiwan Strait. Chinese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:34.000
artists render video game-like scenes of valiant&nbsp;
Chinese soldiers roaming the streets of Taipei.

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.920
This mix of shame, patriotism,&nbsp;
and thirst for conquest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.720
makes a large number of Chinese citizens&nbsp;
love the party just that much more.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:50.160
One can’t help but wonder: Given what&nbsp;
an invasion would entail, is Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:55.280
more useful to the Chinese leadership as a&nbsp;
perpetually imminent national rejuvenation?

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:01.200
A button, in simplistic terms, Chinese authorities&nbsp;
can press, whenever things aren’t going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:08.480
particularly well at home, not only to distract,&nbsp;
but more importantly, to stoke Nationalism?

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:12.880
This does not, however, rule out an invasion.

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:17.920
The danger, for China, is that it can&nbsp;
easily become dependent on this button.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.560
Each time China gains short-term&nbsp;
domestic approval for threatening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:27.040
Taiwan or making outlandish claims&nbsp;
about how easy an invasion would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:32.880
it is, in effect, writing a check that&nbsp;
would be extraordinarily costly to cash.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:39.840
Yet, it has created conditions under which&nbsp;
it may feel forced to — if, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:45.600
Taiwan were to formally declare independence&nbsp;
or if it faced a legitimacy crisis at home.

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:53.280
And, ultimately, predictions can only get&nbsp;
us so far. One of the principal lessons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:58.160
so far from the war in Ukraine is not&nbsp;
that the “experts” were wrong, exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:03.040
but that rational models of how humans&nbsp;
should behave don’t always match reality.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:09.120
We can only see the world as it is,&nbsp;
not as Putin or Xi Jinping see it.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.560
Russia will pay an enormous&nbsp;
economic cost for its actions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:19.440
It has already been reduced on the world&nbsp;
stage to the status of North Korea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:25.920
But, although these sanctions may slow&nbsp;
its efforts, they have not dissuaded them.

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:30.720
While the odds of a Chinese invasion&nbsp;
of Taiwan may appear slim on paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.960
we cannot proceed under that assumption.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:40.320
…In no small part because the world’s vigilance is&nbsp;
itself one of the things that may dissuade China.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:44.640
By taking the possibility seriously,&nbsp;
we may make it less likely.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:53.440
The status quo is ideal for no one. China will&nbsp;
forever see Taiwan as a “renegade province”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:59.040
a piece of unfinished business from its&nbsp;
past. Many in the United States are likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.200
angry that we have to tip-toe around&nbsp;
the “issue” so as not to upset a bully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:08.480
And, of course, the people of&nbsp;
Taiwan face the biggest burden of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:14.480
all — being denied the privileges, safety, and&nbsp;
opportunities of widely-recognized statehood.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:21.120
But, at least for now, all three parties have&nbsp;
seemingly and for vastly different reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.800
decided the status quo is still&nbsp;
better than the alternative.

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:29.440
Still, predicting China’s&nbsp;
actions is hard — in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:33.840
currently harder than it has been for&nbsp;
decades, as it remains effectively cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:37.840
off from the rest of the world thanks to its&nbsp;
extremely heavy-handed COVID restrictions.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:44.320
China is so cut off from the outside world that&nbsp;
it’s now banning all, quote, “unnecessary travel”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:48.720
preventing many of its own citizens, even&nbsp;
students studying abroad, from leaving.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:53.200
Almost no one expects China&nbsp;
to open up again anytime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:58.560
soon. Why? That’s a question that’s&nbsp;
been on my mind for a long time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.480
and one I try to tackle in the second episode&nbsp;
of my new Nebula Original series. Some argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:10.560
it’s about the economy. Others say it’s more about&nbsp;
presenting an image of China abroad. But none of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:16.560
these theories quite capture the craziness we’ve&nbsp;
seen in Shanghai — live fish tested for COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:20.960
investment bankers left starving in their&nbsp;
homes, and all manner of things in between.

